incant/sixShapeT -- BEAR-TRAP #35's SIX SHAPES, MADE RUNNABLE.

Built 2026-09-02 as a STEP 0 CAPTURE for the flip (SEQ 101). Bear-trap #35's
six-way table was measured on 2026-08-28 and written into genLadder/
countPopulation's PROSE; it had no runnable form, so the flip's motion list --
"the three 64s read 39, all six shapes agree" -- had nothing to read.

THE TRAP: a property read INSIDE a compound condition under-filters a walk at
exit 0. Bare-and-positive is correct; negated, compared-to-zero, or dotted in
the condition is wrong; captured-to-a-local-first is correct either way.

⚠ AND `!` IS NOT THE CULPRIT, which is the half a careful reader gets wrong.
`== 0` fails identically, and once the value is in a local BOTH `!` and `== 0`
are fine. The broken thing is reading the property IN THE CONDITION.

⚠ THE FOUR FILTERS ARE countPopulation's, COPIED EXACTLY -- not noPrint, not a
bin, is a rule, actionType 0 -- so this fixture's numbers are directly
comparable to bear-trap #35's table and to the flip's motion list. Without them
the same walk reads 41/65 instead of 39/64: the SPLIT is identical and only the
population differs, which is worth knowing if anyone re-derives it.

PRE-FLIP READING, taken 2026-09-02 before the flip: S1 39, S2 63, S3 63, S4 63,
S5 39, S6 39.

⚠ THE WRONG ARMS READ 63, NOT THE LEDGER'S 64, AND THE -1 IS ACCOUNTED FOR:
`tokenize` retired earlier the same day, which took the rule population down by
exactly one -- the same -1 the genParse odometer took (64 -> 63) in that stroke.
The THREE CORRECT ARMS still read 39 because tokenize was never in the filtered
population they count. A difference that reconciles is not drift.

POST-FLIP EXPECTATION, pre-registered in SEQ 101: ALL SIX READ 39.

⚠ A WALK THAT RETURNS TOO MANY MEMBERS LOOKS EXACTLY LIKE A WALK THAT IS
WORKING, which is why this is worse than an under-walk: 64 is not obviously
wrong, only bigger.

NEXT: OPEN -- this is a gauge, not a citizen. It has no fix to recommend; it
exists so the flip's central claim can be read rather than argued.
